\begin{proof}
Let $\{H_i\mid i\in I\}$ be a nonempty collection of normal subgroups of $G$, and set $K=\bigcap_{i\in I}H_i$.
Since each $H_i$ contains the identity, $e\in K$. If $x,y\in K$, then $x,y\in H_i$ for every $i\in I$, so $xy^{-1}\in H_i$ for every $i\in I$. Hence $xy^{-1}\in K$, and the subgroup criterion shows that $K$ is a subgroup of $G$.
For any $a\in K$ and $g\in G$, we have $a\in H_i$ for every $i\in I$. Because each $H_i$ is normal in $G$, $gag^{-1}\in H_i$ for every $i\in I$. Thus $gag^{-1}\in K$, so $K\trianglelefteq G$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proof}
Let $\{H_i\mid i\in I\}$ be an arbitrary nonempty collection of normal subgroups of $G$, and write $K=\bigcap_{i\in I}H_i$.
For every $i\in I$, $e\in H_i$, hence $e\in K$. If $x,y\in K$, then $x,y\in H_i$ for all $i\in I$. Since every $H_i$ is a subgroup, $xy^{-1}\in H_i$ for all $i\in I$, and therefore $xy^{-1}\in K$. Thus $K$ is a subgroup of $G$.
Now let $a\in K$ and $g\in G$. For every $i\in I$, $a\in H_i$ and $H_i\trianglelefteq G$, so $gag^{-1}\in H_i$. Consequently $gag^{-1}\in K$. Hence $K$ is normal in $G$.
\end{proof}
